WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.240
Policies trained in simulation

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.650
often break when the physics changes.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.780
In this work, we ask what actually matters when

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.690
we adapt a pretrained world model to a new domain,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.970
and we turn the answers into DomainADEPT.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.450
Here is the setup.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.080
The source domain is a simulated copy of our real robot cell.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.110
We train a visual policy there with domain randomization,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.310
and in simulation it threads the needle reliably.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.930
On the real robot, we deploy the same policy.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.470
The scene matches, but friction, contact and actuation do not,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.540
and in a contact-rich insertion task,

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.360
those small differences are enough

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.120
to make it fail much of the time.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.880
Our goal is to close this dynamics gap

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.630
with only about fifty real-world episodes.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.350
Same task, same visuals — but the physics still differs.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.290
Our analysis points to three rules.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.510
Keep the value frozen.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.770
Fix the dynamics with an ensemble.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.390
And tether the proposal policy to the source.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.470
DomainADEPT works in three stages.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:17.950
First, in simulation,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.730
we collect diverse data from expert and sub-optimal policies,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.300
and distill an RL teacher into a latent world model: an encoder,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.570
an ensemble of dynamics heads, a value head, and a policy head.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.030
Second, before any online interaction,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.360
we fine-tune the policy head on twenty

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.190
human demonstrations from the real robot.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.440
Fitting so few demonstrations makes

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.450
the policy forget useful behavior,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.090
so we tether it to the source policy on source states.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.010
Third, on the real robot,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.970
the proposal policy suggests candidate actions,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.520
the dynamics ensemble rolls them out,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.750
and the frozen value scores them.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.660
Only the dynamics are fine-tuned on the new real transitions.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.100
After fifty real episodes,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.400
DomainADEPT reaches one hundred percent on Threading

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.230
and ninety percent on Square,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.290
versus fifty and sixty-five percent for SimDist.
